/**

@page page_peano4_particles_mesh_storage Multiscale particle storage

Particles use a dual-tree association. The replicated spacetree supplies the
multilevel geometry, while each rank stores particle sets only in its relevant
vertex pool slots.

# Level selection

A particle resides on a level whose cell width can accommodate its search
diameter. The association chooses the vertex on that level closest to the
particle centre. Particles with different search radii can therefore occupy
different levels of the same spacetree.

For a particle with search radius @f$r@f$ and level width @f$h@f$, the
association selects a level satisfying the particle model's @f$r@f$ to
@f$h@f$ constraint. The update action sets lift or drop the particle when a
position or radius change invalidates that selection.

# Dual-grid interpretation

The vertex association is equivalent to a cell association on a grid shifted
by half a cell width along every axis. The spacetree vertices form the centres
of these dual cells, or equivalently the sites of a Voronoi partition on each
level.

# Rank ownership

The authoritative particle is local on the rank owning its associated mesh
region. Other ranks can hold virtual boundary copies. A rank-boundary merge
updates these copies with one sweep of lag, and the sieve routes a particle
whose destination is not available in the local vertex pool.

Particle containers make their rank traversal serial, so no
subtree-task particle copies exist within a rank.

*/
